Coding Standard

We will gradually introduce elements of our coding standard in the lab adventures. A coding standard increases readability and allows for easier editing and troubleshooting.

For this lab:

    Imports at the top of the code
    All constants appear below the import(s) in ALL-CAPS
    Make sure you add you/your partner's names, contributions and the date to the comment header
    No line should exceed 99 characters wide. This is why we added the vertical rulers to VSCode. This include comments and print statements.
    Use blank lines to separate logical areas of the code.
    Use descriptive variable names.
    Put a space around every math operator
        ex: x = 5 * 3 - 19 not x=5*3-19


Grading Criteria

Your submission will be graded based on the following criteria:

    Functionality (5 points): The program runs without errors and meets all requirements.
    Code Quality (5 points): Code follows coding standards, is well-organized, includes comments, does not go past 99 characters wide, and all math operators have a space around them
    Mathematical Accuracy (5 points): All calculations are correct, and outputted in the correct format.
    Creativity (5 points): The character introduction and setting description are engaging and creative.


